  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES WV imagery shows a broad mid level troughing pattern \nover central Canada into the Upper Midwest, extending from a broad \nclosed low spinning over Hudson Bay and northern Manitoba/Ontario. \nEmbedded in this broad trough, a shortwave is moving over northern \nMN. At the sfc, a cold front and weak troughing drape southwest \nacross MN. Visible satellite has shown ample clearing much of the \nday ahead of this front, allowing for plenty of warming in the mid \n70s to low 80s. Tds in the mid to upper 60s have helped increase \ninstability with SBCAPE upwards of 1500 J/kg with little to no CIN \nnoted in the latest SPC mesoanalysis. As this boundary progresses \neastward over the UP, shower and storm activity is expected to \nincrease over the west late this afternoon, moving eastward through \nearly tonight. CAMs are struggling with the onset of convection in \nthe CWA. Given the lack of activity currently over the northern end \nof the cold front, a HRRR solution of slightly later timeframe than \npreviously forecast is favored. Likely starting between 4-6 pm \neastern in the west. 0-6 km bulk shear remains unimpressive, but \nsufficient at 30 kts for organized convection. Steeper low level \nlapse rates of at least 7.5 C/km alongside the favored linear mode \nsupport the main threat being gusty winds, however marginal mid \nlevel lapse rates and MUCAPE anticipated to reach 2000 J/kg \nleave a secondary threat of large hail on the table. Moisture is\nalso not impressive, PWATs currently noted between 1.1 to 1.3 \ninches, but southwest moisture advection ahead of the boundary \nis expected to increase this to 1.4 to 1.8 inches (highest east \ninto tonight). Probabilities of greater than 1 inch are between \n10-40%, highest over the central UP. Totals are expected between\n0.25 and 0.75 inches, save for the south central where coverage\nis lower and duration is shorter leading to lower amounts \naround 0.1 inch. These threats are captured well in the WPC \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall UP wide as well as the SPC \nslight risk for the west half of the UP and marginal risk for \nthe remainder of the CWA.\n\nTemperatures tonight range from the upper 40s to low 60s, coldest \ninterior west in the wake of the front. Lingering shower and storm \nactivity is expected to end in the CWA by around sunrise on \nSaturday. High pressure ridging at the sfc yields a dry and pleasant \nday on Saturday with mostly sunny skies and temps peaking in the 70s \nto low 80s. \n\nSunday brings back chances for showers and storms as a cold front \nmoves through. Modest instability and plenty of bulk shear could \nsupport stronger storms, however better instability and forcing for \nsevere storms remains south of the CWA. NBM probabilities of \nexceeding 0.25 inches of rainfall are between 10-40%, highest over \nthe west half of the UP. Otherwise quasi zonal flow over the Upper \nGreat Lakes leads to pleasant summer temps in the 70s the remainder \nof the forecast period with lows in the upper 40s to 50s. Chances \nfor showers and storms dot the extended forecast as transient \nshortwaves traverse the area.",
